## THE FIXED TEXT PARTS (approved byte for byte by Tuesday's read-whole; ASCII except the one `<EMDASH>` marker; one line each; paths and lines at main 746dec44, blobs as pinned)
- `<LABELS>` = ai-assistant, b1100-finding, b1114-2026-11-02
- `<MOVE-D>` = This ticket moves to Done next, under the project's work-tracking rule for a decision with no code change.
- `<MOVE-N>` = This ticket does not move in this record; its status is unchanged.
- `<R919-BLUF>` = The product owner ruled on 2026-11-02 to stay on sqlite3 6.0.1 for now and to name node:sqlite, the driver built into Node, as its successor; nothing is built or changed. This ticket's done-when is met: the written successor assessment of 2026-11-02 lists the candidates with their measures and a recommendation (it sits in the project's Briefs folder and its file name ends SQLITE3-SUCCESSOR-ASSESSMENT.md), and the product owner has chosen to stay on sqlite3 6 and has named the successor. {MOVE-919}
- `<R919-REC>` = Reassess the choice when any one of these four triggers fires, in the wording of the assessment: (a) an advisory against sqlite3 6.0.1, its production install chain, or SQLite 3.52.0 as bundled, with no upstream fix; (b) node:sqlite reaching Stability 2 on the Node line the image uses (read the line in the Node docs for that line; today 24.x prints 1.2); (c) npm enabling default-deny for install scripts without a simple approve entry, or the image build ceasing to be able to reach GitHub release assets; (d) a Node major that changes N-API behaviour so the napi-v6 binding stops loading. If (b) alone fires, moving is optional rather than urgent. If a trigger fires, the assessment's order is: first the preparation that suits any successor (replace the 5 double-quoted SQL literals and route the raw database calls through one module), then swap in node:sqlite behind the adapter the assessment measured, then rewrite the call sites natively, then remove sqlite3; each step is its own pull request with a tier 1 gate, and the Deploy demo run on each merge must read skipped. Nothing in this comment starts any of that.
- `<R919-D1>` = Measured by the assessment on Node 24.22.2 and 26.8.1: sqlite3 6.0.1 passes the 19 characterisation cells of the RD-913 test file and 515 tests across 23 wider suites; npm audit for production dependencies reads 0; GitHub lists 2 advisories for the package, both patched in 5.1.5 or earlier. node:sqlite passed the same 19 cells and 515 tests behind a 93-line adapter with the database module unchanged, but Node labels it Stability 1.2, release candidate, on the 24.x and 26.x lines (1.1 on 22.x), and it does not exist on Node 18 or 20. better-sqlite3 ships its prebuilt binaries yet npm ci still runs a compile step, which failed on node:24-alpine for ARM and x64 unless install scripts are skipped; it also needs the 5 SQL literals edited. The upstream sqlite3 repository is archived (last push 2026-06-27) and its README says DEPRECATED without naming a successor. Removing sqlite3 would remove 46 packages from the lock. A correction to this ticket's description: its figure of 101 distinct statements was not reproduced; the assessment counted 85, 97 or 67 depending on how DDL batches and dynamic UPDATE variants are counted, and it did reproduce the 5 statements that rely on double-quoted string literals.
- `<R919-D2>` = Not tested by the assessment: the server-booting suites, the whole product image, jest cells on Linux, Node 18, 20 and 22 for any candidate, and Windows. Not known: whether an advisory is coming. Related: RD-918 (the install-script entry and the compose path), RD-913 (the upgrade to sqlite3 6). The product owner's ruling is recorded in the project's clarifications file.
- `<R918-BLUF>` = Two measured facts from the successor assessment of 2026-11-02, both about how sqlite3 6.0.1 reaches an install. (1) Part (b) of this ticket is stronger than written: docker/Dockerfile.backend cannot build whatever its Node base is, because its line 8 copies backend/package*.json and no such file is tracked (0 tracked files match; the root package.json is 1), so raising its base image to Node 24 would not make it build. That file is already held by RD-494. (2) Denying the install script was measured to break the driver: after npm install-scripts deny sqlite3, which writes allowScripts with sqlite3 set to false into package.json, npm ci exits 0 with 286 packages, no binding file is present, and require of sqlite3 fails.
- `<R918-REC>` = For part (a), the allow-list entry must approve sqlite3, never deny it; an approve entry is configuration only. For part (b), the compose path is retired or repaired under RD-494; docker/docker-compose.yml also names docker/Dockerfile.frontend (line 8) and docker/Dockerfile.codellama (line 45), and neither is tracked, so the compose path cannot build at all. Nothing in this comment changes anything.
- `<R918-D1>` = Measured by the assessment on 2026-11-02: with npm 11.19 and the product lockfile, an install that skips scripts, or denies this one, leaves no binding file and require fails with the message Could not locate the bindings file. On node:24-alpine (npm 11.19.1, no compiler) the binding arrives as a downloaded release asset of 1,177,655 bytes for ARM and 1,227,708 bytes for x64, equal to the asset sizes GitHub lists, so the image build depends on GitHub release assets at build time. On node:18-alpine the lockfile installs and the binding loads despite the declared engines (node 20.17.0 or later). Files read at main 746dec44.
- `<R918-D2>` = Not known: which npm version first blocks install scripts by default. Not measured: the whole product image (only its dependency stage was built). Related: RD-919, RD-913, RD-494.
- `<W1-SUMMARY>` = backend/simpleDb.js insertPrinterLog resolves undefined instead of the new row id and never finalizes its statement; no production code calls it (low)
- `<W1-BLUF>` = insertPrinterLog in backend/simpleDb.js (line 175) is meant to resolve with the id of the row it inserts, but it resolves undefined. Its statement callback at line 225 is an arrow function, so this.lastID at line 229 reads a property of the SimpleDatabase object and not of the sqlite3 statement; addCostCenter, which uses a function callback, resolves 1. The method also prepares its statement at line 193 and runs it at line 194 without ever finalizing it, so closing the database afterwards logs SQLITE_BUSY about unfinalized statements. Both were measured by the successor assessment of 2026-11-02 and neither is caused by the driver. Nothing fails in production: no production code calls the method (only tests do).
- `<W1-REC>` = Use a function callback, or read the id from the statement the callback is bound to, and finalize the statement in the callback; then add one red-first cell that inserts a row and asserts that the resolved id is a positive number and that closing the database logs no unfinalized-statement warning. A code change in backend/simpleDb.js with its own review gate. Likelihood is low: the method has no production caller.
- `<W1-D1>` = Measured by the successor assessment (a harness drove every public method of the database module): insertPrinterLog resolved undefined; addCostCenter resolved 1; after insertPrinterLog, closing the database logged SQLITE_BUSY: unable to close due to unfinalized statements, the warning path of the close method at lines 424-446 of the same file. Read at main 746dec44, backend/simpleDb.js blob 0d07e81a (876 lines): lines 193, 194, 225 and 229 as named above.
- `<W1-D2>` = Not known: whether a future caller will need the id. Related: RD-919, RD-142 (the earlier fix of the close method, Done), RD-296 (records that the method has no production caller). Done when: a red-first cell asserts a positive id and a clean close and passes; or the product owner has chosen to leave it (the method is unused) and this ticket is closed with the decision recorded.
- `<W2-SUMMARY>` = backend/simpleDb.js getAllPrinterLogs given an offset without a limit builds OFFSET with no LIMIT and rejects with a SQLite syntax error (low)
- `<W2-BLUF>` = getAllPrinterLogs in backend/simpleDb.js (line 271) adds LIMIT only when the caller passes a limit between 1 and 10000 (lines 306 to 312) and adds OFFSET whenever the caller passes an offset (lines 314 to 320). SQLite does not accept OFFSET without LIMIT, so a call with an offset of 1 and no limit rejects with SQLITE_ERROR (near OFFSET: syntax error), while the same call with a limit works. The successor assessment of 2026-11-02 measured the rejection; the SQL is the same on any engine. By reading the code, a limit of 0 or above 10000 is dropped silently and leaves the same shape. Nothing is known to fail today: no call in backend/server.js passes an offset (the word does not occur in that file; the method name occurs 53 times there as a control).
- `<W2-REC>` = Decide the intended behaviour first: add LIMIT -1 when an offset is given without a limit, or reject an offset without a limit with a clear error, or document that an offset needs a limit. Then add one red-first cell for the chosen behaviour. A code change in backend/simpleDb.js with its own review gate. Likelihood is low.
- `<W2-D1>` = Measured by the successor assessment: getAllPrinterLogs with an offset of 1 and no limit rejected with SQLITE_ERROR, near OFFSET, syntax error, the SQL being the text ending ORDER BY created_at DESC OFFSET ?; with a limit it returned rows. Read at main 746dec44, backend/simpleDb.js blob 0d07e81a: the ORDER BY at line 303, the limit block at lines 306-312, the offset block at lines 314-320 with OFFSET ? at line 317.
- `<W2-D2>` = Not known: whether any caller outside backend/server.js (routes, services, scripts) passes an offset. Related: RD-919, RD-139 (names offset among the method's filters). Done when: the intended behaviour is decided and a red-first cell holds it; or the product owner has chosen to leave it and this ticket is closed with the decision recorded.
- `<W3-SUMMARY>` = scripts/clear_db.js awaits calls on the raw sqlite3 handle that return the handle and not a promise, so its clears, its VACUUM and their errors are not waited for (low)
- `<W3-BLUF>` = scripts/clear_db.js clears the demo database with six lines of the form await db.db.run(...) (lines 14, 17, 20, 23, 26 and 30, the last one VACUUM). db.db is the raw sqlite3 handle, and its run without a callback returns the handle itself, not a promise, so each await resolves at once and does not wait for its statement. By reading the code, an error in a statement is raised as an event on the handle and not caught by the try block at lines 7 to 39, and the success lines at 32 and 33 print before the statements are known to have run. The successor assessment of 2026-11-02 found this while counting the callers of the raw handle. It is an offline development script, not run in production.
- `<W3-REC>` = Route the six statements through methods that return promises, or wrap each in a promise that waits for its callback, then close the database after the last one. A cell is not practical for a script, so verify by running it against a scratch database and reading the row count of every table afterwards. A code change in scripts/clear_db.js with its own review gate. Likelihood is low.
- `<W3-D1>` = Read at main 746dec44, scripts/clear_db.js blob 4394b199 (45 lines): the await lines named above, db.close() at line 35 and the try block at lines 7 to 39. That run without a callback returns the handle is the assessment's measurement; the order in which the statements finish was not measured.
- `<W3-D2>` = Not known: whether any run of this script left a table uncleared or reported success early. Related: RD-919, RD-296 (names the script as an offline delete utility). Done when: the script waits for each statement and a run against a scratch database leaves every table empty; or the product owner has chosen to leave it and this ticket is closed with the decision recorded.
